{"records":[{"id":"phk5jhhm7d","title":"Single-cell whole-genome sequencing Burkitt lymphoma","description":"Burkitt lymphoma (BL) carries multiple oncogenic drivers yet arises predominantly in children, whose normal cells harbour few age-related mutations. To investigate this paradox, we sought to define the sequence and timing of mutational drivers underlying BL development. Here, we analysed single-cell whole-genome sequencing (WGS) data of 250 paired normal and malignant B-cells from BL patients and integrated this data with 21 bulk WGS samples and an existing dataset of 157 clonally expanded B-cells from healthy individuals. Phylogenetic reconstruction revealed an accelerated accumulation of mutations following the emergence of the most recent common ancestor, leading to the early establishment of extensive genetic intra-tumoral heterogeneity. We further provide evidence that convergent evolution shapes this diversity at both the point mutation– and copy number variation–levels. This in-depth characterisation further establishes BL as a paradigmatic model of tumorigenesis with implications for therapeutic intervention.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-22T14:23:45.908Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/phk5jhhm7d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/phk5jhhm7d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alexander","last_name":"Steemers","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genomics","Burkitt Lymphoma"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9pd6xdcpfh","title":"Single-cell whole-genome sequencing reveals convergent evolution in Burkitt lymphoma","description":"This depository contains all the post-PTATO filtered mutation calls (VCF files) of single-cells . Burkitt cells and normal B-cells were whole genome sequenced after Primary Template-directed Amplification (PTA)-based whole genome amplification.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-12-05T00:30:14.987Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9pd6xdcpfh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9pd6xdcpfh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Bioinformatics","last_name":"van Boxtel","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pediatrics","Burkitt Lymphoma","Genome Sequencing","Whole Genome Amplification"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"d857z43fjr","title":"KCH Lymphoma Study Burkitt Database","description":"Database of prospective cohort of Burkitt lymphoma patients at Kamuzu Central Hospital, Lilongwe, Malawi. Manuscript published in Blood Advances; Painschab, et al., 2019.\n\nABSTRACT\nBurkitt lymphoma (BL) is common in sub-Saharan Africa (SSA). In high-income countries, BL is highly curable with chemotherapy. However, there are few prospective studies from SSA describing non-pediatric BL and no regional standard of care. Thirty-five participants aged ≥15 years with newly diagnosed BL from 2013-2018 in Malawi were enrolled. Chemotherapy was administered according to local institutional guidelines, with concurrent antiretroviral therapy (ART) if HIV-infected. Median age was 21 (range 15-61) and 15 (43%) participants were HIV-infected. Twenty-seven (77%) participants had stage III/IV disease and 19 (54%) had ECOG performance status (PS) >1. Among HIV-infected, median CD4 count was 130 (range 29-605), and ten (67%) had suppressed HIV viral load. Four (11%) patients died before chemotherapy initiation. First-line chemotherapy consisted of CHOP in 22 (71%), infusional EPOCH in four (13%), high-dose methotrexate-based chemotherapy in four (13%), and rituximab+CHOP in one (3%). Among 28 evaluable participants, fourteen (50%) achieved a complete response and six (21%) achieved a partial response. Median overall survival (OS) was seven months; one-year OS was 40% (95% CI 24-56%). 16/22 (73%) deaths were from disease progression. Death was associated with worse PS, lower BMI, higher stage, higher LDH, and higher creatinine. Compared with CHOP, more intensive chemotherapy was associated with decreased mortality (HR 0.24 [0.05-1.02], p=0.05). This is among the best characterized prospective cohorts of non-pediatric BL in SSA. Most deaths resulted from progressive BL. Patients who received more intensive therapy appeared to have better outcomes. Defining optimal approaches is an urgent priority in SSA. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-01-26T22:47:10.836Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/d857z43fjr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/d857z43fjr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Matthew","last_name":"Painschab","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Human Immunodeficiency Virus","Burkitt Lymphoma"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7gwtb7mgrr","title":"Genomic and Transcriptomic Characterization of NKTCL","description":"Natural-killer/T-cell lymphoma (NKTCL) is an aggressive and heterogeneous entity of non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma, strongly associated with Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) infection. To identify molecular subtypes of NKTCL based on genomic structural alterations and EBV sequences, we performed multi-omics study on 128 biopsy samples of newly diagnosed NKTCL and defined three prominent subtypes.\n\n ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-04-02T03:32:10.22Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7gwtb7mgrr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7gwtb7mgrr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Weili","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genome Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fjhp6pwjwp","title":"High-grade corded and hyalinized endometrioid carcinoma with MSI-high and STK11 mutation: A case report and literature review","description":"This dataset comprises genomic and clinical data from a case of high-grade corded and hyalinized endometrioid carcinoma, a rare and aggressive variant of endometrial cancer.  The tumor exhibits microsatellite instability-high (MSI-H) status and harbors a mutation in the STK11 gene, which is implicated in cellular metabolism and tumor suppression.  The dataset includes whole-exome or whole-genome sequencing data, MSI status analysis, and immunohistochemical profiles.  Clinical information, such as patient demographics, treatment history, and outcomes, is also provided.  This case contributes to the understanding of the molecular landscape of high-grade endometrioid carcinomas and highlights the potential role of STK11 mutations and MSI-H status in tumorigenesis and therapeutic response.  The data is intended to support further research into targeted therapies and biomarker discovery for rare endometrial cancer subtypes.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-03-17T15:58:33.994Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fjhp6pwjwp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fjhp6pwjwp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xiru","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fengyun","last_name":"Cui","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yingxue","last_name":"Yuan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hongxia","last_name":"Cheng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhixin","last_name":"Cao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Pathology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"g4r22w9pp8","title":"Evolutionary origin of Group 3/4 medulloblastoma","description":"This repository contains variant calls from bulk whole genome sequencing of medulloblastomas that were first published in Northcott, P. A. et al. The whole-genome landscape of medulloblastoma subtypes. Nature 547, 311-317 (2017); https://doi.org/10.1038/nature22973 and re-analyzed for reconstructing evolutionary dynamics driving medulloblastoma initiation in the manuscript\n\nKey oncogenic event are late-stage drivers of medulloblastoma evolution and progression\n\nKonstantin Okonechnikov1,2,3*, Piyush Joshi1,2,3,4*, Verena Körber5,6*, et al.\n\n1.\tHopp Children’s Cancer Center (KiTZ), Heidelberg, Germany\n2.\tNational Center for Tumor Diseases (NCT), NCT Heidelberg, a partnership between DKFZ and Heidelberg University Hospital, Germany\n3.\tDivision of Pediatric Neurooncology, German Cancer Research Center (DKFZ) and German Cancer Consortium (DKTK), Heidelberg, Germany\n4.\tDevelopmental Origins of Pediatric Cancer Junior Research Group, German Cancer Research Center (DKFZ), Heidelberg, Germany.\n5.\tDivision of Theoretical Systems Biology, German Cancer Research Center (DKFZ), Heidelberg, Germany\n6.\tMRC Molecular Hematology Unit, Weatherall Institute of Molecular Medicine, Radcliffe Department of Medicine, University of Oxford, UK\n\n* these authors contributed equally\n\nThe repository additionally contains reference data used for the analysis in Okonechnikov, Joshi & Körber et al.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-02-17T17:53:18.414Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/g4r22w9pp8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/g4r22w9pp8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Verena","last_name":"Körber","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Konstantin","last_name":"Okonechnikov","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Piyush","last_name":"Joshi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stefan","last_name":"Pfister","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lena","last_name":"Kutscher","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Medulloblastoma","Genome Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"j8sr945yfs","title":"OFC_IRF6_Case-Control_Kerala","description":"The Genome analysis Data is taken from the Whole Exome Sequencing of 100 controls and 100 orofacial cleft cases from Kerala Population. The IRF6 Polymorphisms were extracted from the original WES files to Microsoft Excel sheets for analysis. Orofacial Cleft Subtypes and gender of the individuals participated in the study is included with the data.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-01-20T15:33:44.178Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/j8sr945yfs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/j8sr945yfs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alex","last_name":"George","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Reema Rose","last_name":"Alappat","orcid":"0000-0001-7189-8186"}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"Smile Train India","grant_id":"JMH/2021/07"},{"id":null,"name":"Council of Scientific & Industrial Research (CSIR)","grant_id":"08/724(0006)/2019-EMR-I"}],"categories":["Genetics","Genotype","Data Analysis","Association Analysis","Haplotype"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cyp2f9h8nd","title":"Quantum biology of DNA base mispair illuminates its whole-genome landscape in cancer genome","description":"Primary data","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-22T17:09:17.462Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cyp2f9h8nd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cyp2f9h8nd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Qian","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Mutation","Genome"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"69my4hnj2y","title":"Exhaustive circulating tumor DNA sequencing reveals genomic landscape of Hodgkin lymphoma and facilitates ultrasensitive residual disease detection","description":"Supplementary tables for manuscript: \"Exhaustive circulating tumor DNA sequencing reveals genomic landscape of Hodgkin lymphoma and facilitates ultrasensitive residual disease detection\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-12-19T18:15:43.781Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/69my4hnj2y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/69my4hnj2y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sven","last_name":"Borchmann","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genomics","Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"65dnysxym8","title":"Mutation calls by SMM-Seq of MutaMouse male liver exposed to Benzo(B)Fluoranthene for 28 days","description":"MutaMouse males were exposed to five doses of Benzo(B)Fluoranthene (BbF) alongside vehicle controls by oral gavage for 28 days with a 3 day sampling time. Random whole-genome sequencing of liver DNA was performed using MutagenTech's SMM-Seq. Each sample was sequenced to roughly 200Mb. Data is single-nucleotide variants identified by SMM-Seq for 24 mouse liver samples. Variants identified by SMM-seq were contrasted against the single-nucleotide polymorphisms database (dbSNP) to remove known mouse variants. Additionally, germline variants were excluded through conventional whole-genome sequencing of matched samples at 20X depth. Mutation frequency (number of mutations divided by total read depth) is significantly increased by BbF at the top two doses compared to control. BbF increases proportions of C>A subtypes compared to control.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-19T14:57:23.972Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65dnysxym8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65dnysxym8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Annette","last_name":"Dodge","orcid":"0000-0002-0446-9055"},{"first_name":"David","last_name":"Schuster","orcid":"0009-0001-6316-4358"},{"first_name":"Alexander","last_name":"Maslov","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chris","last_name":"Bradley","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Matthew","last_name":"Meier","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Toxicology","DNA Sequencing","Mouse Model","Polycyclic Aromatic Hydrocarbon"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":134283,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":123,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":134283,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":133753,"restricted":64,"embargoed":466},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2306},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1331},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":646},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":470},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":435},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":420},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":409},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":377},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":306},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":273}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":131651},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1903},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":185},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":134},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":74},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":57},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":41},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],